  "campus_description": "ATL@ATLANTA",
  "course_description": "This course explores one of a wide range of topics pertaining to the African American experience in the fields of human and civil rights, social and literary texts, and the social sciences.",
  "course_notes": "(Same as REL 370 1) (Historically, the media has misrepresented Haitian Vodou as a malevolent tradition of zombies, poison, and \"voodoo dolls.\" In reality, Haitian Vodou and African Vodún are religions based on principles of healing, balance, and ancestral devotion. Students will analyze media portrayals such as The Princess and the Frog and Lovecraft Country as well as \"pop\" journalism to understand how they reinforce racist stereotypes of African-derived traditions. Students will also examine real Vodou ethnographies by scholar-practitioners, addressing 21st century issues like gender and queerness, Protestant conversion, Haitian ancestral gifts, and Euro-American initiates. Ultimately, students will learn how to discern the differences between the media's distortions and the reality of Vodou as a powerful African heritage religion today.)",
